---
import katex from 'katex';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
import LabShell from '../../../components/scientific-systems/LabShell.astro';
import RecordedLu from '../../../components/scientific-systems/RecordedLu.astro';
import InteractiveEngine from '../../../components/scientific-systems/InteractiveEngine.astro';
import { labProjects, numericalResults, symbolicResults, type LabProject } from '../../../data/scientific-systems';
import examples from '../../../data/scientific-symbolic-fixtures.json';
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return labProjects.filter(project => project.detail).map(project => ({ params: { id: project.id }, props: { project } }));
}
interface Props { project: LabProject; }
const { project } = Astro.props;
const numerical = project.id === 'numerical';
const render = (formula: string) => katex.renderToString(formula, { throwOnError: true, trust: false, output: 'htmlAndMathml' });
---
<LabShell title={project.title} description={project.summary}>
  <header class="lab-detail-header"><a class="lab-back" href="/labs/scientific-systems/">← Scientific Systems Lab</a><p class="lab-kicker">PROJECT {project.number} / {project.discipline}</p><span class="lab-status lab-active">{project.status}</span><h1>{project.title}.</h1><p>{project.summary}</p><p class="lab-source-note">C++20 · CMake · GoogleTest · interactive release 29 October 2026<br /><a href={project.sourceUrl}>Public C++ source and build instructions ↗</a></p></header>
  {numerical ? <>
    <p class="lab-source-note">Dated 26 October validation snapshot. The public native release adds two QR tests; current CI results are linked from its repository.</p>
    <div class="lab-stats"><div class="lab-stat"><strong>{numericalResults.testEntries}/21</strong><span>passing CTest entries</span></div><div class="lab-stat"><strong>{numericalResults.solveCases}</strong><span>NumPy solve reference cases</span></div><div class="lab-stat"><strong>3.56 × 10⁻¹⁶</strong><span>maximum sampled scaled backward error</span></div></div>
    <section class="lab-two-column"><div><h2>Implemented boundary.</h2><ul class="lab-list">{project.implemented.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul><p>Real binary64, column-major matrices. LU accepts rectangular inputs; solves require square factors. The solve API leaves factors and right-hand sides unchanged and returns a dense solution for every RHS.</p></div><div class="lab-architecture"><p class="lab-kicker">ALGORITHM / AX = B</p><h3>Permute. Substitute. Solve.</h3><p>Given PA = LU, apply the same forward pivot swaps to B, solve LY = PB with an implicit unit diagonal, then solve UX = Y by backward substitution.</p><p class="lab-mono">Validated shape, storage and pivots<br />Exact singularity → domain error<br />Nonfinite solve arithmetic → overflow error</p></div></section>
    <InteractiveEngine kind="numerical" />
    <RecordedLu />
    <section class="scientific-section" id="evidence"><p class="lab-kicker">BINARY EVIDENCE / SEPARATE FROM NEW SOLVE LOGIC</p><h2>Recovered flow, explicit adaptations.</h2><p>REA/Ghidra inspected the LAPACK DLL shipped with GNU Octave 11.3.0 for Windows. Recursive DGETRF2 control flow informed LU. Scalar BLAS replacements, checked C++ boundaries and the new triangular solver are independently implemented.</p><div class="lab-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Observed native functions" tabindex="0"><table class="lab-table"><caption>Static analysis virtual addresses in the selected PE x86-64 DLL; these are not observed runtime addresses.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Function</th><th scope="col">Address</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DGETRF2</td><td><code>0x2e066ace0</code></td><td>Recursive panels and pivot offsets</td></tr><tr><td>DLAMCH</td><td><code>0x2e07e4770</code></td><td>Safe minimum constant</td></tr><tr><td>DLASWP</td><td><code>0x2e06c2a80</code></td><td>Sequential row swaps</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="lab-mono">Target SHA-256: 5132bafddc40afdedaa6e4ad7e72cce27595f4b395cff9cbc3332f4b19cc0c9d<br />DGETRF2 evidence: ev_d5506c16009862d2a35427eecb73ebf07fecb1eaef88690f16ac9501a6316a14</p><p class="lab-note">Original-DLL differential execution remains unperformed. REA found additional BLAS, XERBLA, GCC and GFortran runtime dependencies; an integrity-verified dependency closure is not yet available. The larger Octave wrapper search previously exceeded 900 seconds.</p></section>
    <section class="scientific-section"><p class="lab-kicker">VALIDATION / RESIDUALS AND REFERENCE VALUES</p><h2>Accuracy needs a defined question.</h2><p>Tests cover singularity, pivoting, padded storage, overflow, multiple-RHS consistency, 323 seeded rectangular LU matrices and ill-conditioned examples. An additional 98-case solve run compared against NumPy 2.3.5: maximum absolute solution difference 2.78 × 10⁻¹⁷. The scaled backward error uses ‖AX − B‖∞ / (‖A‖∞‖X‖∞ + ‖B‖∞).</p><p>Small residuals do not prove forward accuracy for ill-conditioned matrices. INFO = 0 is not a condition estimate. Finite LU inputs can still produce nonfinite intermediates.</p></section>
    <section class="scientific-section" id="benchmarks"><p class="lab-kicker">MEASUREMENTS / RECORDED 25 OCT 2026</p><h2>An application-level baseline.</h2><p>Previous LU + signed-logdet timings, seven samples per size after warmup, requested one BLAS thread. MSVC 19.44, Ryzen 9 8945HS, Windows x64, NumPy 2.3.5. C++ includes copying, validation, factorization and logdet; NumPy includes its own API overhead. These are neither isolated-kernel timings nor measurements of Octave's DLL.</p><div class="lab-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Recorded LU benchmark results" tabindex="0"><table class="lab-table"><caption>Median microseconds, not a portable speed guarantee. New solve timing is not included.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Matrix order</th><th scope="col">Scalar C++</th><th scope="col">NumPy reference</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[[32,15,13.6],[64,72.5,35.4],[128,457.3,159.6],[256,3865.5,1039.1]].map(row => <tr>{row.map(value => <td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><a class="text-link" href="/media/scientific-systems/validation-summary.json">Download metrics, raw timing samples and reproduction commands ↗</a><p class="lab-source-note">Recorded measurements date from 25 October; current engine source and documented toolchain are available in the public repository.</p></section>
  </> : <>
    <p class="lab-source-note">Dated 26 October validation snapshot. The current native engine adds two checked-trace tests; the full local suite passes 12 entries, and CI runs 11 native entries.</p>
    <div class="lab-stats"><div class="lab-stat"><strong>{symbolicResults.testEntries}/10</strong><span>passing CTest entries</span></div><div class="lab-stat"><strong>366</strong><span>independent reference cases</span></div><div class="lab-stat"><strong>Exact</strong><span>integer and rational arithmetic</span></div></div>
    <section class="lab-two-column"><div><h2>Small enough to reason about.</h2><ul class="lab-list">{project.implemented.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul><p>Signed dynamically sized base-10⁹ integers, positive coprime rational denominators, immutable expression trees, precedence-aware parsing and deterministic printing. No GMP runtime dependency.</p></div><div class="lab-architecture"><p class="lab-kicker">PARSE → TRANSFORM → VALIDATE</p><h3>Keep structure and meaning distinct.</h3><p>Structural equality compares trees. Bounded polynomial normalization proves supported identities. Agreement at sampled values answers a different question and does not prove equivalence.</p><p class="lab-mono">Integer powers · exact fractions<br />Explicit domain conditions<br />Unknown outside the identity checker’s scope</p></div></section>
    <InteractiveEngine kind="symbolic" />
    <section class="lab-example"><p class="lab-kicker">RECORDED NATIVE OUTPUT / NOT A LIVE CAS</p><h2>A derivative carries its assumptions.</h2><p>Expand these examples exported by the C++ CLI. KaTeX presents the original formula; differentiation was performed by the native engine, not by KaTeX or browser JavaScript.</p>{examples.map((example, i) => <details open={i === 0}><summary>{example.input}</summary><div class="lab-formula" set:html={render(example.latex)} /><pre>Canonical: {example.canonical}<br />Derivative with respect to x: {example.derivative}<br />Original-domain conditions: {example.nonzeroConditions.length ? example.nonzeroConditions.map(condition => `${condition} ≠ 0`).join('; ') : 'none within the supported exact domain'}</pre></details>)}</section>
    <section class="scientific-section"><p class="lab-kicker">CHECKED MATHEMATICS / HONEST LIMITS</p><h2>Never erase a domain restriction.</h2><p>The core keeps x/x and 0·(1/x) unsimplified where cancellation could erase the undefined point x = 0. Under this engine’s convention 0⁰ is undefined, so differentiating x⁰ returns zero with the original condition x ≠ 0.</p><p>Polynomial identity checking is exact within its bounded expansion scope. Variable or fractional-power differentiation is unsupported. The parser accepts explicit operators and integer literals, with no implicit multiplication or function calls. Input length, tree height, exponents and expansion work have documented resource limits.</p><p class="lab-note">This is an independently designed symbolic engine. It does not reproduce proprietary Wolfram algorithms or claim general theorem proving.</p></section>
    <section class="scientific-section"><p class="lab-kicker">REA RESEARCH / ARITHMETIC BACKEND</p><h2>A narrow compiled comparison point.</h2><p>REA/Ghidra recovered <code>__gmpq_canonicalize</code> at <code>0x2599fa290</code> from the installed Git distribution’s GMP library. The routine adjusts a negative denominator sign, branches on a zero denominator field, calls a GCD function and conditionally divides both operands.</p><p>GMP is an arithmetic backend, not a complete CAS. The observed eight-byte limbs and allocation strategy differ from our base-10⁹ implementation. No GMP numerical code was executed or differentially compared.</p><p class="lab-mono">PE x86-64 · package metadata: GMP 6.3.0-2<br />SHA-256: 37248cda33845e7c5c35a574e57da762dd0a8268f0270d179f30a33746809681<br />Evidence: ev_ea6e4c84c3bdb84a6b115ea09c966cc6b4b6dc2e94e326c9c8779d062750af2c</p></section>
    <section class="scientific-section"><h2>Independent exact validation.</h2><p>200 large signed-integer cases use Python integers; 100 rational cases use Fraction; 66 symbolic cases compare simplification and derivatives with SymPy 1.14.0. Separate native tests check parsing, round trips, domain restrictions, equivalence and LaTeX escaping. Selected identities passing does not establish general symbolic correctness.</p><a class="text-link" href="/media/scientific-systems/validation-summary.json">Read the dated validation snapshot ↗</a></section>
  </>}
  <section class="scientific-section"><p class="lab-kicker">NEXT MILESTONE</p><h2>{project.next}</h2><p>{project.limitation}</p><a class="text-link" href="/labs/scientific-systems/">Explore the full lab roadmap ↗</a></section>
</LabShell>
